                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_70\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit changes a single line in BookView.java's page-number lookup logic: `int pageNum = 0;` -> `int pageNum = -1;` inside the method that computes the page number for a given spine index/position (\"Looking for pageNumber for index=..., position=...\"). The bug report states the title page incorrectly gets reported as page-num 0 instead of a sentinel value.\n\nTracing this method back through the candidates: it did not exist in BookView.java originally. It was first introduced in candidate_70, as `getPageNumberFor(int index, int position)` in `PageTurnerSpine.java`, with the exact same initialization `int pageNum = 0;` and the same loop structure that is later fixed:\n\n    public int getPageNumberFor( int index, int position ) {\n        int pageNum = 0;\n        if ( index >= pageOffsets.size() ) {\n            return -1;\n        }\n        for ( int i=0; i < index; i++ ) {\n            pageNum += pageOffsets.get(i).size();\n        }\n        List<Integer> offsets = pageOffsets.get(index);\n        for ( int i=0; i < offsets.size() && offsets.get(i) < position; i++ ) {\n            pageNum++;\n        }\n        return pageNum;\n    }\n\nWhen index=0 and position=0 (the title page), neither loop executes, so pageNum remains at its initial value of 0 \u2014 exactly the bug described (\"Title-page has page-num 0 again\"). This method was later moved/refactored into BookView.java (confirmed by subsequent candidates such as candidate_157, which show `int pageNum = 0;` already present as unchanged context when the surrounding logging/refactoring was added), carrying the same flawed initialization forward until the fix commit finally changed the default to -1.\n\nSince candidate_70 is the first commit (chronologically) to introduce this initialization pattern and the associated loop logic that fails to distinguish \"no page yet\" from \"page 0\", it is the bug-introducing commit.\n"
                }
